\chapter{Graphs, Terms and Reduction}

\texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} and \texttt{M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d} provide a directed multigraph, first-order terms, term rewriting, and sharing-preserving term graph reduction. The feature tags are \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}s}, \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, and \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}. As with \texttt{E\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}, the types and functions are also usable independently of feature composition. C++ names live in \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l}; D names are module-level.

\section{Directed graphs}

In C++, construct \texttt{D\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>}; both payload types default to \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. In D, use \texttt{n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}V\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})}; both default to \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. D graphs are reference types. C++ graphs are value types. These mutable objects are not synchronized.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
DirectedGraph<> graph;
auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
auto edge = graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
auto path = graph.shortest_path(entry, exit);
assert(path && path->size() == 2);
std::string dot = graph.to_dot("Control flow");
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}d} and \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}d} are graph-local integer handles. Handles are never recycled; removal invalidates only the removed handles. Using an invalid handle throws. Handles from different graphs must not be mixed. Parallel edges, self-loops, cycles, and isolated nodes are supported. Edge insertion order is preserved in adjacency lists, including when an incoming edge is redirected.

