Traversals, shortest paths, topological sorting, and strongly connected components take linear time in visited vertices and edges, apart from scanning retired storage slots for whole-graph operations. Node and edge enumeration and counts scan the corresponding storage. Deletion removes entries from adjacency arrays. References returned by C++ accessors can be invalidated by subsequent graph mutations; retain IDs rather than references across mutations.

\section{DOT output}

\texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{})} returns complete directed Graphviz DOT text, including isolated nodes and parallel edges. Internal IDs such as \texttt{n\allowbreak{}0} are used as DOT identifiers; payloads become quoted labels. Quotes, backslashes, and line breaks are escaped. Tabs are retained as literal tabs. Other ASCII control characters are rejected. Output order is deterministic for the same mutation history.

The default renderer uses stream insertion in C++ and \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}!\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} in D. For arbitrary payloads, pass label callbacks:

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto dot = graph.to_dot(
    [](const std::string& value) { return "node: " + value; },
    [](const std::string& value) { return value; },
    "Annotated graph");
std::ofstream("graph.dot") << dot;
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{d}{}
\begin{MDCode}
import MuDSL;
import std.file : write;

auto graph = new DirectedGraph!();
auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
write("graph.dot", graph.to_dot("Control flow"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

DOT generation has no external dependency. To turn the text into an image, install Graphviz and run \texttt{d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}g}. The toolkit does not launch Graphviz or write files implicitly.

\section{First-order terms}

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m} is a structural value with a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l}, ordered \texttt{a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e}. Variables and nullary function symbols are distinct. Both implementations copy terms deeply. D exposes \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} for copying a const term into a mutable value.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto variable = Term::variable("x");
auto zero = Term::function("0");
auto sum = Term::function("add", {variable, zero});
assert(sum == parse_term("(add ?x 0)"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

D uses \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})} because \texttt{f\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n} is a D keyword. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m} uses the existing S-expression reader. Unquoted \texttt{?\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e} atoms are variables; quoted \texttt{"\allowbreak{}?\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}"} atoms are literal symbols. Function heads must be literal atoms. Empty lists, variable heads, empty variable names, and variables with arguments are rejected. Symbols may contain spaces or punctuation; dumping quotes them as needed. There is no global signature table: the same function name can appear at different arities, but matching and unification require both name and arity to agree.

\texttt{d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} serialize terms; \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} converts the other way. \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} returns a set of bare variable names (a \texttt{b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}]} map in D), \texttt{g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} tests for absence of variables, and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} counts tree nodes. \texttt{a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} selects a subterm using zero-based argument indices; the empty position selects the root. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns a new term and leaves the original unchanged. Invalid positions throw.

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n} maps bare variable names to terms. \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}j\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns optional bindings and enforces structural equality at repeated variables. Subject variables are treated as subject data, not as additional pattern variables. A failed match publishes no partial bindings.

\texttt{s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})} performs simultaneous substitution; absent variables remain unchanged and inserted bindings are not recursively substituted again. \texttt{u\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns an optional most general unifier with an occurs check. Its bindings are composed so one simultaneous substitution makes both input terms equal. It rejects cyclic equations such as \texttt{?\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}?\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{})}. Optional results use \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} in C++ and \texttt{N\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} (\texttt{i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}t}) in D.

\section{Term rewrite systems}

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} contains \texttt{n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e}, \texttt{l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s}. Rules require a nonvariable left side and bind every right-side variable on the left. \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m} validates and owns copies of its rules. \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} accepts a \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} or an S-expression string; \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} parses a standalone rule. Supported forms are \texttt{(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}, \texttt{(\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}, and \texttt{(\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
TermRewriteSystem rules;
rules.add_rule("(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)");
rules.add_rule("(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)");
auto report = rules.normalize(parse_term("(mul (add a 0) 1)"), 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 2);
assert(report.term == parse_term("a"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

\texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{})} returns an optional \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p} containing the entire rewritten \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m}, \texttt{r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x}, and redex \texttt{p\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n}. Rules are tried in insertion order at each position. \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} searches root before children; \texttt{I\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} searches children before root. Both visit arguments from left to right. D uses enum access with \texttt{.} instead of \texttt{:\allowbreak{}:}.

\texttt{n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns a \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} with \texttt{t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p}. \texttt{R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m} means no rule matches anywhere; \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t} means another rewrite is available but the budget is exhausted. A zero budget only checks reducibility. A term that reaches normal form on the last allowed step reports \texttt{N\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m}. Identity rules count as rewrites and exhaust the budget rather than being silently discarded. Termination and confluence are not assumed or proved.

\section{Shared term graphs and graph reduction}

\texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} stores ordered, acyclic term graphs using \texttt{D\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}>}. Its graph view is read-only so the term shape and acyclicity invariants cannot be broken by ordinary callers. Nodes carry a symbol and variable flag; edges carry zero-based argument indices.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
TermRewriteSystem rules;
rules.add_rule("(= (add ?x 0) ?x)");
TermGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(pair (add a 0) (add a 0))");
auto report = graph.reduce(root, rules, 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 1);
assert(graph.to_term(report.root) == parse_term("(pair a a)"));
graph.collect_garbage({report.root});
std::ofstream("reduced.dot") << graph.to_dot();
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

