\texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{})} reuses existing nodes with equal labels and identical child handles. With \texttt{s\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}, each occurrence gets its own nodes. \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{})} explicitly constructs a function node with existing children and never merges it. These construction operations cannot introduce cycles. \texttt{g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} exposes traversal and read APIs; \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} unfolds a graph into a tree; \texttt{t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{})} renders all live nodes. Each sharing-enabled \texttt{a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}d} builds a hash index of live nodes and interns the input bottom-up. Rebuilding the index keeps it consistent with reductions and edge redirections between additions.

Graph matching operates directly on node handles. Repeated variables compare subgraphs structurally even when they are not physically shared. When a rule fires, right-side variables reuse their bound nodes. Every incoming edge of the redex is redirected to the replacement, so all occurrences of a shared redex change in one reduction. The redex node is removed. Newly introduced function nodes are fresh; rewriting does not automatically maximize sharing.

\texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{})} returns an optional \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p} with \texttt{r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x}, \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}, and \texttt{r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x}. \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{})} returns \texttt{G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t} with \texttt{r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}s}, and \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}p}. Only nodes reachable from the selected root are searched. The same rule priority, strategy, and budget contracts apply as for term rewriting, with each shared node visited once per search.

Always continue with the returned root: rewriting the root removes its old ID. Other external root handles that equal a removed redex must also be updated using the step's replacement. Incoming edges from other roots are redirected too. Garbage is retained until \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{})}; supply every root you want to keep. Empty roots remove all nodes. Invalid roots throw before collection removes anything. Remaining handles stay stable.

The general graph supports cycles; \texttt{T\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} deliberately represents finite acyclic terms. Step budgets limit rewrites, not memory or recursion depth. Tree operations recurse through terms, and unfolding a highly shared graph can produce an exponentially larger tree. These APIs do not implement higher-order binding, cyclic term reduction, completion, or equality saturation; the separate \texttt{E\allowbreak{}G\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h} API remains available for equality saturation.

\section{Verification}

The graph and term regression tests cover both implementations. The standalone test project includes D tests automatically when \texttt{l\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}2}, \texttt{d\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}d}, or \texttt{g\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}c} is available; use \texttt{-\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}M\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}L\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}E\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}D\allowbreak{}=\allowbreak{}O\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}F} to disable that target.

